{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The direct shape pairs up the ends. The **reduction** shape is more powerful: each comparison eliminates not just one pair, but an entire row of possibilities at once."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "It requires one thing — the array must be **sorted**."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Why sorting changes everything"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "On an unsorted array, knowing that `arr[left] + arr[right]` is too large tells you nothing. Any other pair might be larger or smaller; you have learned nothing you can act on."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "On a sorted array, that same comparison is decisive."
    },
    {
      "type": "drawing",
      "src": "/drawings/323d3b8d-6ac4-4986-a156-2936228b57a2.svg",
      "alt": "On a sorted array, comparing the pair sum to the target tells you which pointer to move",
      "caption": "Sorted order turns the comparison into a decision about which pointer moves",
      "width": 880,
      "height": 300,
      "scene": {
