    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "If the sum is too big, the only way to shrink it is a smaller value — and since `right` holds the largest remaining number, moving it inward is the single correct move. If the sum is too small, move `left` outward for a bigger number."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The elimination, counted"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Here is what actually makes this fast. When you move `right` inward, you are not discarding one pair. You are discarding **every** pair that used `arr[right]` — because `arr[left]` is already the smallest available value, and even that was too big."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "So each step removes an entire row of the N x N grid of pairs. N steps, N rows eliminated, whole grid covered. That is how **O(N²)** becomes **O(N)**."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The skeleton"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "arr.sort((a, b) => a - b);\n\nlet left = 0;\nlet right = arr.length - 1;\n\nwhile (left < right) {\n  const sum = arr[left] + arr[right];\n\n  if (sum === target) return [left, right];\n  if (sum < target) left++;    // need bigger\n  else right--;                // need smaller\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Watch the cost of sorting"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "If the input arrives sorted, the whole thing is O(N) with O(1) space. If you sort it yourself, sorting costs **O(N log N)** and that dominates — the two pointer scan becomes the cheap part."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "This is worth comparing against a hash map, which solves the unsorted version in O(N) time but O(N) space. Two pointers wins on memory; the hash map wins on time when the input is unsorted. Knowing which constraint matters is the real skill."
    }
